WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:04.330
Bees might not be your
favorite insects out there.

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:06.950
After all, their stings can really hurt.

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:07.783
Ouch!

00:00:07.783 --> 00:00:09.964
But as far as important species go,

00:00:09.964 --> 00:00:12.350
bees are near the top of the list.

00:00:12.350 --> 00:00:15.105
You see, bees are critical pollinators,

00:00:15.105 --> 00:00:16.338
meaning they're responsible

00:00:16.338 --> 00:00:18.854
for pollinating more
than a third of the crops

00:00:18.854 --> 00:00:20.990
that feed the world's population.

00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:24.530
But today, bees are dying
off at record rates.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:27.760
So, what would a world
without bees look like,

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.473
and what would that mean
for our global food supply?

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:35.637
Well, if all the bees on
Earth suddenly dropped dead

00:00:35.637 --> 00:00:39.360
tomorrow, things wouldn't
look good for our planet.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.710
And that's because pollination is critical

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.885
to the health of our global ecosystem.

00:00:43.885 --> 00:00:45.768
It's what allows plants to reproduce

00:00:45.768 --> 00:00:47.921
and grow the fruits and
vegetables humans need

00:00:47.921 --> 00:00:49.472
for a balanced diet.

00:00:49.472 --> 00:00:51.222
And the thing is, bees are some of

00:00:51.222 --> 00:00:53.005
the best pollinators out there.

00:00:53.005 --> 00:00:56.113
In fact, they've coevolved
with flowering plants

00:00:56.113 --> 00:00:59.650
over millions of years to
become pollinating machines.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:01.808
Elina Niño: Of course,
a lot of them are fuzzy,

00:01:01.808 --> 00:01:03.766
so they have hairs,

00:01:03.766 --> 00:01:06.990
or specialized equipment, so to speak,

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.820
where they can store pollen.

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:10.545
Narrator: Bees help pollinate the reported

00:01:10.545 --> 00:01:14.223
84% of crops humans eat
that are insect-pollinated.

00:01:14.223 --> 00:01:16.523
Globally, this accounts for a staggering

00:01:16.523 --> 00:01:20.156
$235 billion to $577 billion

00:01:20.156 --> 00:01:22.800
in annual food production.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.014
Without bees and other pollinators,

00:01:25.014 --> 00:01:26.897
supermarket shelves would hold about

00:01:26.897 --> 00:01:29.408
half the fruits and
vegetables they have now,

00:01:29.408 --> 00:01:32.216
transforming the produce
section from this...

00:01:32.216 --> 00:01:33.610
to this.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:36.230
We wouldn't have luxuries
like almonds, apples,

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:38.240
or avocados.

00:01:38.240 --> 00:01:42.490
Niño: We would also suffer
in terms of dairy production.

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:47.130
We use alfalfa to feed dairy cattle,

00:01:47.130 --> 00:01:50.380
and dairy cattle, of
course, produces the milk,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:51.930
and the milk is used to produce

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:54.484
many dairy products that we eat.

00:01:54.484 --> 00:01:56.132
When I talk to the kids, all the kids

00:01:56.132 --> 00:02:00.040
are very disappointed to hear
that we might lose ice cream.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:02.343
Narrator: The extinction of
bees could have a disastrous

00:02:02.343 --> 00:02:05.751
domino effect, killing off
animals that eat those plants,

00:02:05.751 --> 00:02:07.383
and so on up the food chain.

00:02:08.270 --> 00:02:11.067
Luckily, humanity wouldn't
face a global famine

00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:12.480
like you might expect.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.498
Niño: We wouldn't
necessarily starve to death,

00:02:15.498 --> 00:02:17.763
because there are a lot
of plants that we do eat

00:02:17.763 --> 00:02:20.613
and we depend on that are wind-pollinated,

00:02:20.613 --> 00:02:23.060
including, for example, wheats.

00:02:23.060 --> 00:02:25.865
Narrator: So if the bee
apocalypse does hit,

00:02:25.865 --> 00:02:27.804
we could still meet
the daily calorie needs

00:02:27.804 --> 00:02:29.587
of our global population.

00:02:29.587 --> 00:02:32.687
Our diets, however, would
suffer in major ways,

00:02:32.687 --> 00:02:35.380
as foods that provide key
nutrients for our bodies

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.220
would become scarce and
extremely expensive.

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.947
Niño: A lot of the plants that we eat

00:02:39.947 --> 00:02:42.730
that are dependent on animal pollination

00:02:42.730 --> 00:02:45.267
do provide the necessary micronutrients

00:02:45.267 --> 00:02:46.834
that improve our health.

00:02:46.834 --> 00:02:48.858
We would probably be very sickly.

00:02:48.858 --> 00:02:52.500
So, for example, let's just
think about citrus, right?

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:56.182
We would probably have issues with scurvy,

00:02:56.182 --> 00:02:57.990
if you think about it.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.565
I guess you could really
supplement that with vitamins,

00:03:00.565 --> 00:03:03.895
but again, definitely
not all of the vitamins

00:03:03.895 --> 00:03:07.560
can be as easily accessible to the body

00:03:07.560 --> 00:03:10.360
as they are when you're
actually eating them.

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:12.808
Narrator: According to the
US Department of Agriculture,

00:03:12.808 --> 00:03:15.285
the work that bees do
for US farmers is worth

00:03:15.285 --> 00:03:19.270
about $15 billion a year, so without them,

00:03:19.270 --> 00:03:22.340
the cost of produce would skyrocket.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.082
Niño: Especially those who are
socioeconomically challenged,

00:03:26.082 --> 00:03:30.346
they already have hard times
accessing the healthy foods,

00:03:30.346 --> 00:03:33.090
so this would probably
impact them even worse.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.770
Narrator: And we could be
closer to a bee-less future

00:03:35.770 --> 00:03:37.053
than you might expect.

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:41.140
In 2018, American
beekeepers reported losing

00:03:41.140 --> 00:03:43.573
41% of their colonies,

00:03:43.573 --> 00:03:45.567
and worldwide, bees have been dying off

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.990
at record rates for the past decade.

00:03:47.990 --> 00:03:51.963
Niño: Honeybees, at least in
the past, I believe, 12 years

00:03:51.963 --> 00:03:54.565
have been declining about

00:03:54.565 --> 00:03:59.410
up to 45% annually, in some cases.

00:03:59.410 --> 00:04:01.833
Narrator: Scientists haven't
pinned down an exact reason

00:04:01.833 --> 00:04:04.300
for this sudden decline
in the bee population.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:07.902
However, likely reasons
are global warming,

00:04:07.902 --> 00:04:11.335
overuse of pesticides, and
parasitic varroa mites,

00:04:11.335 --> 00:04:13.863
which spread viruses to bee colonies.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.290
Right now, countries around
the world are working

00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:19.592
to monitor bee colonies and even create

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:22.680
new pollination methods using robots.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:25.096
Niño: There are researchers

00:04:25.096 --> 00:04:28.896
who are trying to develop
drone pollinators,

00:04:28.896 --> 00:04:31.654
so using drone technology

00:04:31.654 --> 00:04:34.100
to pollinate flowers.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:38.503
It all would most likely be more costly.

00:04:40.010 --> 00:04:41.862
Narrator: So, what can
the average person do

00:04:41.862 --> 00:04:43.790
to be more bee-friendly?

00:04:43.790 --> 00:04:46.485
Well, if you have a garden,
plant a range of flowers

00:04:46.485 --> 00:04:48.705
so wandering bees can
have access to nectar

00:04:48.705 --> 00:04:49.688
throughout the year.

00:04:49.688 --> 00:04:52.230
Niño: Bees that we have
that are wild bees,

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:53.946
that are native bees,

00:04:53.946 --> 00:04:57.596
a lot of them actually nest in soil,

00:04:57.596 --> 00:05:02.015
so leaving bare ground for
those bees is really useful

00:05:02.015 --> 00:05:05.448
if you're redoing your lawn, for example.

00:05:05.448 --> 00:05:08.423
Providing access to water would also

00:05:08.423 --> 00:05:10.868
be very helpful for the bees,

00:05:10.868 --> 00:05:14.400
and reducing pesticide
input if at all possible.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.116
Narrator: While steps
are already being taken

00:05:16.116 --> 00:05:18.107
to save the world's bee population,

00:05:18.107 --> 00:05:19.531
there's still a lot to be done

00:05:19.531 --> 00:05:22.373
to protect Earth's most buzzy pollinators.

